# Untitled

In situ decomposition of metal-organic frameworks into ultrathin nanosheets for the oxygen evolution reaction
Kai He\(^{1}\), Zhen Cao\(^{2}\), Ruirui Liu\(^{2}\), Ya Miao\(^{2}\), Houyi Ma\(^{1}\), and Yi Ding\(^{2}\) (✉)
\(^{1}\)Key Laboratory of Colloid and Interface Chemistry of State Education Ministry, School of Chemistry and Chemical Engineering, Shandong University, Jinan 250100, China  \(^{2}\)Tianjin Key Laboratory of Advanced Functional Porous Materials, School of Materials Science and Engineering, Tianjin University of Technology, Tianjin 300384, China
© Tsinghua University Press and Springer-Verlag Berlin Heidelberg 2016
## KEYWORDS
oxygen evolution reaction, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), in situ decomposition, ultrathin nanosheets, electrocatalyst
## ABSTRACT
The oxygen evolution reaction (OER) is a pivotal process for water-splitting and many other energy technologies involving oxygen electrodes. Herein, a new synthesis strategy is proposed to prepare OER catalysts based on a simple yet flexible in situ decomposition of Co-based acetate hydroxide metal-organic frameworks (MOFs). This process allows straightforward fabrication of various 2D hydroxide ultrathin nanosheets (UNSs) with excellent component controllability. The as-obtained Co-based hydroxide UNSs demonstrate superior catalytic activity for the OER due to the exposure of numerous active sites. In particular, the CoNi hydroxide UNSs exhibit low overpotentials \((\eta)\) of 324 and \(372\;\mathrm{mV}\) at current densities of 10 and \(100\;\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\), respectively; a large turnover frequency (TOF) of \(0.16\;\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) at \(\eta=380\;\mathrm{mV}\); and a small Tafel slope of \(33\;\mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{dec}^{-1}\) in an alkaline environment. Importantly, these values are superior to those of the state-of-the-art \(\mathrm{IrO}_{2}\) commercial electrocatalyst. This facile strategy enables the exploration of more efficient and economic OER electrocatalysts with various constituents and opens a promising avenue for large-scale fabrication of functional nanocatalysts for use in clean energy technologies.
## 1 Introduction
High \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) emissions and an ever-increasing demand of fossil fuels are intensifying the global energy crisis and environmental deterioration, compelling us to develop sustainable and eco-friendly energy technologies [1–3]. Electrochemical water-splitting into clean \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) fuel plays an important role in the hydrogen
economy, as it provides an attractive alternative to the use of fossil fuels by enabling efficient energy storage and conversion from renewable sources such as solar and wind energies [4–7]. During this process, the key anodic reaction, namely, the oxygen evolution reaction (OER), is kinetically sluggish and particularly efficiency-limiting. The four-electron redox process often requires a high overpotential to achieve O–H bond breaking
and O–O bond formation, severely impeding its widespread application [8–11]. To this end, the development of highly active OER catalysts has been the subject of continuous research efforts. It has been found that some state-of-the-art precious-metal electrocatalysts and their oxides such as \(\mathrm{IrO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{RuO}_{2}\) have excellent OER activity. However, their high cost, scarcity, and rapid deactivation hinder their use [12, 13]. Among the OER catalysts that are potential substitutes for these precious-metal catalysts, earth-abundant transition-metal oxides (TMOs) and their derivatives have become the main research focus due to their diversity, eco-friendliness, low cost, and favorable overpotential [14, 15]. Based on the volcano-like relation model [16], cobalt-based compounds such as \(\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{O}_{y}\) [17, 18], CoPi [19], \(\mathrm{NiCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) [20–22], \(\mathrm{Zn}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{3-x}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) [23], and Co-based double hydroxides [24–26] have been found to be promising candidates [27].
Two-dimensional (2D) ultrathin TMO nanomaterials, which have large active surface areas and thus a large number of active sites due to the exposure of more metal ions [28, 29], provide an optimal solution, as their short ion diffusion length and larger contact area with the electrolyte are conducive to fast interfacial charge transfer and efficient electrochemical reactions [30–33]. In particular, previous studies have shown that layered complexes of Co-based hydroxides or oxyhydroxides composed of edge-sharing octahedral \(\mathrm{MO}_{6}\) layers possess excellent OER catalytic activity [34–36].
Inspired by the above consideration, we herein highlight a facile synthesis strategy for obtaining 2D Co-based hydroxide ultrathin nanosheets (UNSs) based on a simple liquid decomposition of Co-based acetate hydroxide metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) at room temperature. MOFs have been very intensively studied in the past decade, and their functional structural properties have attracted great interest for a wide range of applications [37–40]. Unfortunately, some MOFs are unstable in acidic or alkaline aqueous solution. Taking advantage of this instability, we demonstrate that Co-based acetate hydroxide MOFs with a 3D layered network structure can serve as a unique precursor for 2D Co-based hydroxide UNSs with excellent catalytic activity for the OER. Furthermore, the components of the MOF precursors can be controlled by
replacing the coordination metal ions by other metal ions with the same coordination mode, leaving the overall MOF structure unchanged [41–45]. Accordingly, CoZn and CoNi hydroxide UNSs can also be generated to optimize the electrical conductivity of the catalysts and increase the number of catalytic active sites. Among the various samples produced herein, CoNi hydroxide UNSs were found to exhibit the best catalytic activity for the OER, with low overpotentials \((\eta)\) of 324 and \(372\;\mathrm{mV}\) at current densities \((j)\) of 10 and \(100\;\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\), respectively, and, more importantly, a large turnover frequency (TOF) of \(0.16\;\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) at \(\eta=380\;\mathrm{mV}\) and a small Tafel slope of \(33\;\mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{dec}^{-1}\), outperforming the commercial \(\mathrm{IrO}_{2}\) electrocatalyst.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Materials
Cobalt(II) acetate tetrahydrate \(\left(\mathrm{C}_{4}\mathrm{H}_{6}\mathrm{CoO}_{4}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},\right.\) AR), nickel(II) acetate tetrahydrate \(\left(\mathrm{C}_{4}\mathrm{H}_{6}\mathrm{NiO}_{4}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},\right.\) AR), zinc(II) acetate dihydrate \(\left(\mathrm{C}_{4}\mathrm{H}_{6}\mathrm{ZnO}_{4}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},\right.\) AR), potassium hydroxide (KOH, AR), and ethanol \(\left(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{OH},\right.\) AR) were purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co. Ltd. Commercial \(\mathrm{IrO}_{2}\) nanoparticles (NPs) were obtained from Alfa Aesar, and \(5\;\mathrm{wt.}\%\) Nafion solution was purchased from DuPont. All chemicals were used as received without further purification. Ultrapure water (resistivity \(>18\;\mathrm{M}\Omega{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}\)) was used throughout the experiments.
## 2.2 Synthesis of Co-based hydroxide UNSs
To prepare the MOF precursors, 5 mmol of cobalt(II) acetate tetrahydrate or 5 mmol of a 2:1 (molar ratio) mixture of cobalt(II)/zinc(II) or cobalt(II)/nickel(II) acetate hydrates was dissolved in \(50\;\mathrm{mL}\) of ethanol and then refluxed at \(80\;^{\circ}\!C\) for \(3\;\mathrm{h}\). The as-prepared MOF precursors were centrifuged, rinsed thoroughly with ethanol three times, and dried in a vacuum oven at \(60\;^{\circ}\!C\). To synthesize the Co-based hydroxide UNSs, the MOF precursors were added to a \(0.1\;\mathrm{M}\) KOH aqueous solution and then fully ultrasonicated for liquid exfoliation. The products were centrifuged and rinsed thoroughly with water three times and then dried in a vacuum oven at \(30\;^{\circ}\!C\) for subsequent characterization and electrochemical tests.
## 2.3 Characterization
2.3 CharacterizationThe morphologies of the as-prepared Co-based products were investigated using a ZEISS MERLIN compact field emission scanning electron microscope (FE-SEM). The compositions of the hydroxide layered nanosheets (LNSs) were analyzed using an Oxford X-MaxN energy-dispersive X-ray spectrometer (EDS). The microstructures were characterized using a JEOL JEM-2100 transmission electron microscope (TEM) at \(200\,\mathrm{kV}\). Atomic force microscopy (AFM) was conducted using a Bruker INNOVA scanning probe microscope (SPM) in tapping mode. X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns were recorded by a Rigaku Dmax-2500 diffractometer using Cu Kα radiation \((\lambda=0.154\,\mathrm{nm})\). The chemical states of the elements in the samples were explored with X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, PHI 5,000 VersaProbe).
## 2.4 Electrochemical measurements
2.4 Electrochemical measurementsThe electrochemical tests were performed in an \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\)-saturated 1 M KOH alkaline electrolyte using an electrochemical analyzer (CHI760D Instruments), a Compact Pine Rotator (CPR, Pine Instruments), and a three-electrode system including \(\mathrm{Hg/HgO}\) (1 M KOH) with a double salt bridge as the reference electrode and a spiral platinum wire as the counter electrode. To fabricate the working electrode, \(4\,\mathrm{mg\cdot mL^{-1}}\) electrocatalyst with \(5\,\mathrm{wt.\%}\) Nafion solution in a 1:1 (v/v) water/ethanol mixed solvent was ultrasonicated for at least \(1\,\mathrm{~h~}\) to form a uniformly dispersed ink. The glassy carbon (GC) disk electrodes were polished with gamma alumina polishing powder, thoroughly cleaned with ultrapure water and ethanol, and completely dried under ambient conditions. Next, a \(10\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) dispersion was carefully dropped onto the dried GC disk electrode with a surface area of \(0.196\,\mathrm{cm}^{2}\). To reduce the influence of solution resistance, the original data were corrected with \(90\%\) iR compensations in the testing process. Using this three-electrode system, the as-obtained OER catalysts were subjected to linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) at a scan rate of \(5\,\mathrm{mV\cdot s^{-1}}\). The Tafel slopes were calculated from the LSV curves at a current density range of \(1{-}10\,\mathrm{mA\cdot cm^{-2}}\). The chronoamperometric responses were recorded at a potential of \(0.684\,\mathrm{V}\) (vs. \(\mathrm{Hg/HgO}\)). The tested potentials
vs. \(\mathrm{Hg/HgO}\) for the OER at any given \(\mathrm{pH}\) were converted to the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) scale according to the Nernst equation: \(E_{\mathrm{RHE}}=E_{\mathrm{Hg/HgO}}+0.059\times\mathrm{pH}+0.098\).
The TOF values were calculated using Eq. (1)
\[
\mathrm{TOF}=(j\times a)/(4\times n\times F)
\]
where \(j\) is the measured current density at a given overpotential, \(a\) is the surface area of the electrode \((0.196\,\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) for the GC disk electrode), 4 is the number of electrons transferred during \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) production, \(n\) is the mole number of metal ions on the electrode, and \(F\) is the Faraday constant \((F=96.485\,\mathrm{C\cdot mol^{-1}})\).
## 3 Results and discussion
Because the components of the MOF precursors can be easily controlled by mixing other elements with the same coordination mode via simple refluxing of the mixed metal acetate salts, various Co-based acetate hydroxide MOF precursors, including Co, CoZn, and CoNi acetate hydroxide MOFs, were obtained. These precursors all presented a nanoblock morphology
with a relatively narrow size distribution, as demonstrated by the SEM images in Figs. S2(a)–S2(c) (in the ESM). The Co-based hydroxide LNSs (see Figs. S2(d)–S2(f) in the ESM) were obtained from a facile in situ decomposition of Co-based acetate hydroxide MOF precursors in alkaline aqueous solution in a reaction vessel at room temperature. The advance of this structural phase transformation gradually generates \(\mathrm{Co(OH)}_{2},\mathrm{Zn}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{1-x}(\mathrm{OH})_{2},\) and \(\mathrm{Ni}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{1-x}(\mathrm{OH})_{2},\) as proved by the XRD patterns and EDS spectra (see Figs. S2(g)–S2(i) and S3 in the ESM). Upon further continuous ultrasonication, Co-based hydroxide LNSs were successfully exfoliated without additional reagents, and the corresponding UNSs exhibit no obvious compositional change in the Co, Ni, and Zn species (see Figs. S2(g)–S2(l) in the ESM). The XRD patterns displayed in Fig. 1(b) exhibit two sets of characteristic peaks, which are assigned to the (003) and (006) planes of Co-based hydroxide UNSs. These patterns indicate that the interlayer distances are \(70\%\) larger than those of the corresponding unexfoliated hydroxides, exposing more active sites to OER electrocatalysis [34]. Importantly, this synthesis strategy is well suited for large-scale production.
The architecture and detailed geometrical structure of the as-synthesized Co hydroxide UNSs with increased interlayer spacing were intuitively determined from the TEM and AFM images, as displayed in Fig. 2. Notably, the initial hydroxide LNSs were delaminated into many fully exfoliated hydroxide UNSs, which have extremely weak contrast due to their ultrathin nature. This is in line with their highly \((00n)\) -oriented XRD patterns (see Fig. 1(b)). To validate the complete exfoliation of the hydroxide UNSs, Co hydroxide UNSs were dispersed on mica sheets for AFM analysis. The images and corresponding height profiles are shown in Figs. 2(b1)–2(b4) and 2(c1)–2(c4), respectively. The hydroxide UNSs were \({\sim}1.7\pm0.1\ \mathrm{nm}\) thick, further confirming the ultrathin nature of the NSs. The above results clearly show that the \(2\mathrm{D}\,\mathrm{MO}_{6}\) networks are easily exfoliated along the \((00n)\) lattice planes and retain the basic UNS structure. This structural feature favors the exposure of more metal ions, which can act as highly active sites for OER electrocatalysis [30]. In addition, as presented in Fig. S4 (in the ESM), the ultrathin nature of these NSs is confirmed by the TEM and AFM results for the as-prepared CoNi and CoZn hydroxide UNSs exfoliated
from the CoNi and CoZn acetate hydroxide MOF precursors, respectively.
XPS measurements were implemented to determine the chemical identity and detailed chemical and electronic states of the Co \(2\mathrm{p}\), Ni \(2\mathrm{p}\), and Zn \(2\mathrm{p}\) species in the as-prepared hydroxide UNSs. Both the Co \(2\mathrm{p}\) (see Fig. 3(a)) and Ni \(2\mathrm{p}\) (see Fig. 3(b)) signals were split into \(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) and \(2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}\) doublets because of spin-orbit coupling [46, 47]. For the Co \(2\mathrm{p}\) core-level spectrum, two states of Co were detected. The characteristic peaks at the binding energies of 797.0 and \(781.0\,\mathrm{eV}\) are identified as \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\), while the two other peaks at the binding energies of 795.6 and \(779.8\,\mathrm{eV}\) are assigned to \(\mathrm{Co}^{3+}\) [47–49]. The presence of the \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) oxidation state is further indicated by the presence of satellite (sat.) peaks at 802.2 and \(785.6\,\mathrm{eV}\) [50]. The Ni \(2\mathrm{p}\) signals, corresponding to \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) (872.6 and \(855.0\,\mathrm{eV}\)) and \(\mathrm{Ni}^{3+}\) (874.4 and \(856.4\,\mathrm{eV}\)), also exhibited a doublet structure, as shown in Fig. 3(b) [51, 52]. \(\mathrm{Co}^{3+}\) and \(\mathrm{Ni}^{3+}\) have been considered to be the catalytic active sites for the OER [53–56]. In the Zn \(2\mathrm{p}\) spectrum (see Fig. 3(c)), the main peaks for \(2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}\) and \(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\), situated at 1,044.4 and \(1,021.2\,\mathrm{eV}\), respectively, are characteristic of \(\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}\) [57, 58]. The XPS spectra for the Co species of CoNi and CoZn hydroxide UNSs, displayed in Fig. S5 (in the ESM), demonstrate the existence of \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) and \(\mathrm{Co}^{3+}\) in these hydroxide UNSs. In addition, the elemental analyses of the relative atomic ratios (at.%) of CoNi and CoZn hydroxide UNSs determined through XPS measurements (see Table S1 in the ESM) are in good
agreement with the corresponding EDS results (see Fig. S2 in the ESM).
The catalytic performances of the as-prepared Co, CoZn, and CoNi hydroxide UNSs and commercial \(\mathrm{IrO}_{2}\) NPs (see Fig. S6 in the ESM for a typical SEM image) as anode materials for the OER were determined by LSV in 1 M KOH solutions at the same loading of \(0.2\,\mathrm{mg}\cdot\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\). As shown in Fig. 4(a), the onset potentials of both CoNi hydroxide UNSs and Co hydroxide UNSs are \(1.48\,\mathrm{V}\) vs. RHE, which is \(0.1\,\mathrm{V}\) lower than that of CoZn hydroxide UNSs. However, among all the samples, the CoNi hydroxide UNSs exhibit markedly faster growth of the anodic current density \((j)\). For instance, the difference in the overpotentials \((\Delta\eta)\) between CoNi and Co hydroxide UNSs is \(12\,\mathrm{mV}\) at \(j=10\,\mathrm{mA}\cdot\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) \((\eta_{\mathrm{Co\,hydroxide\,UNSS}}=336\,\mathrm{mV},\) \(\eta_{\mathrm{CoNi\,hydroxide\,UNSS}}=324\,\mathrm{mV}\), see Table 1), but increases to \(29\,\mathrm{mV}\) at \(j=100\,\mathrm{mA}\cdot\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) \((\eta_{\mathrm{Co\,hydroxide\,UNSS}}=401\,\mathrm{mV},\) \(\eta_{\mathrm{CoNi\,hydroxide\,UNSS}}=372\,\mathrm{mV})\). In addition, the improved catalytic activity of the CoNi hydroxide UNSs is also reflected by their markedly low Tafel slope, \(33\,\mathrm{mV}\cdot\mathrm{dec}^{-1}\). This value is lower than those of the Co hydroxide UNSs \((41\,\mathrm{mV}\cdot\mathrm{dec}^{-1})\), the CoZn hydroxide UNSs \((40\,\mathrm{mV}\cdot\mathrm{dec}^{-1})\), and, more importantly, the precious \(\mathrm{IrO}_{2}\) NPs \((58\,\mathrm{mV}\cdot\mathrm{dec}^{-1})\) (see Fig. 4(b)). This finding indicates that the CoNi hydroxide UNSs are the most suitable catalyst for electrochemical water-splitting. Supposing that all the metal ions are available for the OER, the apparent TOF can be calculated at different overpotentials, reflecting the OER catalytic activity of
various anodes. As displayed in Fig. 4(c), the TOFs of the CoNi hydroxide UNSs increase markedly faster than those of other samples. At an overpotential of \(380\,\mathrm{mV},\) the TOF of the CoNi hydroxide UNSs reaches a high value of \(0.16\;\mathrm{s}^{-1},\) which is at least twice as high as those of the Co hydroxide UNSs \((0.06\;\mathrm{s}^{-1})\) , CoZn hydroxide UNSs \((0.07\;\mathrm{s}^{-1})\) , and commercial \(\mathrm{IrO}_{2}\) NPs \((0.07\;\mathrm{s}^{-1})\) . This performance is easily one of the highest values reported for an OER catalyst (see Table S2 in the ESM). The exposure of a larger number of active sites could be responsible for the higher intrinsic activity of CoNi hydroxide UNSs. The TEM results show that CoNi hydroxide UNSs tend to break into smaller fragments than the Co and CoZn hydroxide UNSs, which significantly increases their edge lengths. These nanosheet edges are expected to contain unsaturated coordination sites, which may act as the main active sites for the OER [34]. In addition, the synergistic effect of Co and Ni, which enhances the electrical/ionic conductivity and enriches redox reactions involving various ions [59–61], likely plays a key role in the OER. Here, the combined effect of the redox reactions of Co and Ni hydroxides may be responsible for the differences in the redox current characteristic peaks [25, 34, 47, 62] (see Fig. S7 in the ESM). Furthermore, CoNi hydroxide UNSs also exhibit greater stability, with no decline in \(j\) observed after over 10,000 s (see Fig. 4(d)) at a constant potential of \(1.58\;\mathrm{V}\) (vs. RHE) in \(O_{2}\) -saturated 1 M KOH. The higher \(j\) values observed for CoNi hydroxide UNSs may be attributed to a possible effect of the Ni and anode treatment in which more Co(IV) species are generated and proceed to form \({\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{I V}}){-}{\mathsf{O}},\) which is conducive to the OER [26, 63–65].
## 4 Conclusions
In summary, we develop a synthesis strategy for 2D Co-based hydroxide UNSs by utilizing the instability and high component controllability of Co-based acetate hydroxide MOFs. The as-prepared Co-based hydroxide UNSs show excellent catalytic performance for the OER. In particular, CoNi hydroxide UNSs exhibit low overpotentials of 324 and \(372\;\mathrm{mV}\) at \(j=10\) and \(100\;\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2},\) , respectively, and a significantly
larger TOF of \(0.16\;\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) at \(\eta=380\;\mathrm{mV},\) with a small Tafel slope of \(33\;\mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{dec}^{-1}\) in an alkaline medium. Notably, these values are superior to those of the state-ofthe-art \(\mathrm{IrO}_{2}\) electrocatalyst. While this strategy can be readily extended to the design of diverse MOF precursors with different constituents, its high operability enables us to design a wide variety of functional metal hydroxide and oxide electrodes applicable to the OER as well as many other advanced energy technologies such as water-splitting and \(\mathrm{CO}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction reactions.
